---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "EV Charging at Home: Full Electrical Planning Guide | Ampstowatt";
const description = "Planning to install a Level 2 EV charger? Find out exactly what wire gauge, breaker size, and power capacity you need for 240V charging.";
const h1Title = "EV Charging at Home: Full Electrical Planning Guide";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/blog/ev-charging-at-home-electrical-guide/"
  h1Title={h1Title}
  kicker="EV Planning"
>
  <section class="card article-content">
    <h1>{h1Title}</h1>
    <p>Installing an Electric Vehicle (EV) charger at home is one of the most demanding electrical loads a residential panel will ever see. Unlike a dryer or an oven that cycles on and off, an EV charger draws maximum power continuously for many hours.</p>

    <h2>Level 1 vs. Level 2 Charging</h2>
    <p>Understanding the difference between the two main types of home charging is the first step in planning your electrical needs.</p>

    <h3>Level 1 Charging (120V)</h3>
    <p>This is the "trickle charge" you get by plugging your car into a standard wall outlet.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Voltage:</strong> 120V</li>
      <li><strong>Amps:</strong> Usually limited to 12A (on a standard 15A circuit)</li>
      <li><strong>Power:</strong> 1,440 Watts (1.44 kW)</li>
      <li><strong>Charge Rate:</strong> About 3-5 miles of range per hour</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Level 2 Charging (240V)</h3>
    <p>Level 2 chargers require a dedicated 240V circuit, similar to an electric stove or central AC. This is the standard for modern home EV charging.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Voltage:</strong> 240V</li>
      <li><strong>Amps:</strong> Ranging from 16A to 48A (or even 80A for dual chargers)</li>
      <li><strong>Power:</strong> 3.8 kW to 11.5 kW</li>
      <li><strong>Charge Rate:</strong> About 15-45 miles of range per hour</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sizing Your Breaker and Wire (The 80% Rule)</h2>
    <p>Because EV charging takes several hours, the National Electrical Code (NEC) classifies it as a <strong>continuous load</strong>. This means your charger can only operate at 80% of the circuit breaker's rated capacity.</p>
    
    <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);">
          <th>Breaker Size</th>
          <th>Max Charging Amps</th>
          <th>Max Charging Power (240V)</th>
          <th>Typical Wire Gauge (Copper)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">20 Amp</td>
          <td>16 Amp</td>
          <td>3,840 Watts (3.8 kW)</td>
          <td>12 AWG</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">30 Amp</td>
          <td>24 Amp</td>
          <td>5,760 Watts (5.7 kW)</td>
          <td>10 AWG</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">40 Amp</td>
          <td>32 Amp</td>
          <td>7,680 Watts (7.6 kW)</td>
          <td>8 AWG</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">50 Amp</td>
          <td>40 Amp</td>
          <td>9,600 Watts (9.6 kW)</td>
          <td>6 AWG</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;">60 Amp (Hardwired only)</td>
          <td>48 Amp</td>
          <td>11,520 Watts (11.5 kW)</td>
          <td>6 AWG or 4 AWG</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Plug-in vs. Hardwired Chargers</h2>
    <p>A plug-in Level 2 charger usually requires a NEMA 14-50 receptacle. The NEC requires GFCI protection for 240V receptacles in garages. However, many EV chargers have built-in GFCI, which can cause "nuisance tripping" when paired with a GFCI breaker.</p>
    <p><strong>The Solution:</strong> Hardwiring the charger directly to the panel avoids the need for an expensive GFCI breaker and a heavy-duty receptacle, and is the only way to charge at 48 amps (since standard residential plugs max out at 50A breaker / 40A charging).</p>

    <div style="background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--brand-orange); padding: 1rem; margin: 2rem 0;">
      <strong>Calculate your exact needs:</strong> Use our <a href="/ev-charger-amps-to-watts/">EV Charger Amps to Watts Calculator</a> to determine exactly how much power your charger will draw, and our <a href="/wire-gauge-calculator/">Wire Gauge Calculator</a> to ensure your run from the panel to the garage uses properly sized conductors.
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
